INSERT INTO `ai_messages` VALUES (31,11,'user','Can you explain the main idea from Welcome and Learning Roadmap in simple terms?',18,'2026-07-22 16:38:54'),(32,11,'assistant','The central idea is to turn a large Python Programming: Zero to Projects problem into smaller steps: define the goal, apply one concept at a time, check the result, and improve it using feedback.',52,'2026-07-22 16:42:54'),(33,11,'user','Can you give me a practical example?',10,'2026-07-22 16:46:54'),(34,11,'assistant','Start with a small scenario from the lesson. Write down the desired outcome, create a first solution, test it against two clear criteria, then document one improvement for the next version.',45,'2026-07-22 16:50:54'),(35,12,'user','Can you explain the main idea from Welcome and Learning Roadmap in simple terms?',18,'2026-07-22 16:38:55'),(36,12,'assistant','The central idea is to turn a large Modern HTML, CSS and JavaScript problem into smaller steps: define the goal, apply one concept at a time, check the result, and improve it using feedback.',52,'2026-07-22 16:42:55'),(37,12,'user','Can you give me a practical example?',10,'2026-07-22 16:46:55'),(38,12,'assistant','Start with a small scenario from the lesson. Write down the desired outcome, create a first solution, test it against two clear criteria, then document one improvement for the next version.',45,'2026-07-22 16:50:55'),(39,13,'user','Can you explain the main idea from Welcome and Learning Roadmap in simple terms?',18,'2026-07-22 16:38:55'),(40,13,'assistant','The central idea is to turn a large React and Vite in Production problem into smaller steps: define the goal, apply one concept at a time, check the result, and improve it using feedback.',52,'2026-07-22 16:42:55'),(41,13,'user','Can you give me a practical example?',10,'2026-07-22 16:46:55'),(42,13,'assistant','Start with a small scenario from the lesson. Write down the desired outcome, create a first solution, test it against two clear criteria, then document one improvement for the next version.',45,'2026-07-22 16:50:55'),(43,14,'user','Can you explain the main idea from Welcome and Learning Roadmap in simple terms?',18,'2026-07-22 16:38:55'),(44,14,'assistant','The central idea is to turn a large React Native: Build and Ship problem into smaller steps: define the goal, apply one concept at a time, check the result, and improve it using feedback.',52,'2026-07-22 16:42:55'),(45,14,'user','Can you give me a practical example?',10,'2026-07-22 16:46:55'),(46,14,'assistant','Start with a small scenario from the lesson. Write down the desired outcome, create a first solution, test it against two clear criteria, then document one improvement for the next version.',45,'2026-07-22 16:50:55'),(47,15,'user','Can you explain the main idea from Welcome and Learning Roadmap in simple terms?',18,'2026-07-22 16:38:55'),(48,15,'assistant','The central idea is to turn a large Applied Machine Learning with Python problem into smaller steps: define the goal, apply one concept at a time, check the result, and improve it using feedback.',52,'2026-07-22 16:42:55'),(49,15,'user','Can you give me a practical example?',10,'2026-07-22 16:46:55'),(50,15,'assistant','Start with a small scenario from the lesson. Write down the desired outcome, create a first solution, test it against two clear criteria, then document one improvement for the next version.',45,'2026-07-22 16:50:55'),(51,16,'user','Can you explain the main idea from Welcome and Learning Roadmap in simple terms?',18,'2026-07-22 16:38:55'),(52,16,'assistant','The central idea is to turn a large SQL for Analytics problem into smaller steps: define the goal, apply one concept at a time, check the result, and improve it using feedback.',52,'2026-07-22 16:42:55'),(53,16,'user','Can you give me a practical example?',10,'2026-07-22 16:46:55'),(54,16,'assistant','Start with a small scenario from the lesson. Write down the desired outcome, create a first solution, test it against two clear criteria, then document one improvement for the next version.',45,'2026-07-22 16:50:55'),(55,17,'user','Can you explain the main idea from Welcome and Learning Roadmap in simple terms?',18,'2026-07-22 16:38:55'),(56,17,'assistant','The central idea is to turn a large Web Application Security problem into smaller steps: define the goal, apply one concept at a time, check the result, and improve it using feedback.',52,'2026-07-22 16:42:55'),(57,17,'user','Can you give me a practical example?',10,'2026-07-22 16:46:55'),(58,17,'assistant','Start with a small scenario from the lesson. Write down the desired outcome, create a first solution, test it against two clear criteria, then document one improvement for the next version.',45,'2026-07-22 16:50:55'),(59,18,'user','Can you explain the main idea from Welcome and Learning Roadmap in simple terms?',18,'2026-07-22 16:38:56'),(60,18,'assistant','The central idea is to turn a large Product Management Fundamentals problem into smaller steps: define the goal, apply one concept at a time, check the result, and improve it using feedback.',52,'2026-07-22 16:42:56'),(61,18,'user','Can you give me a practical example?',10,'2026-07-22 16:46:56'),(62,18,'assistant','Start with a small scenario from the lesson. Write down the desired outcome, create a first solution, test it against two clear criteria, then document one improvement for the next version.',45,'2026-07-22 16:50:56'),(63,19,'user','Create a study plan',NULL,'2026-07-22 17:14:12'),(64,19,'assistant','Here’s a flexible **2-week study plan** you can use for most subjects. Adjust the daily time based on your schedule—aim for **60–90 minutes per day**.\n\n## Week 1: Learn and Build Understanding\n\n**Day 1 — Plan & Diagnose**\n- List the topics you need to study.\n- Identify what feels easy, medium, and difficult.\n- Set a clear goal, such as: “Understand Chapters 1–4 and solve 30 practice questions.”\n\n**Day 2 — Topic 1**\n- Review notes, lesson material, or a textbook section.\n- Write a short summary in your own words.\n- Complete a few practice questions.\n\n**Day 3 — Topic 2**\n- Learn the next topic.\n- Create flashcards for key terms, formulas, or definitions.\n- Spend 15 minutes reviewing Topic 1.\n\n**Day 4 — Topic 3**\n- Study a new topic.\n- Work through examples without looking at the answers first.\n- Review flashcards from Days 2–3.\n\n**Day 5 — Practice Day**\n- Solve mixed questions from Topics 1–3.\n- Mark mistakes and write down why each mistake happened.\n\n**Day 6 — Difficult Areas**\n- Focus on the topics you found hardest.\n- Rewatch lessons or reread explanations.\n- Teach the concept aloud as if explaining it to someone else.\n\n**Day 7 — Weekly Review**\n- Do a short self-test without notes.\n- Review all summaries and flashcards.\n- Plan what needs more attention next week.\n\n## Week 2: Practice, Recall, and Prepare\n\n**Day 8 — Topic 4 / Next Unit**\n- Study the next major topic.\n- Make notes and complete practice questions.\n- Review earlier flashcards.\n\n**Day 9 — Application**\n- Practice using the concepts in problems, essays, coding tasks, or case studies.\n- Focus on showing your reasoning, not just getting answers.\n\n**Day 10 — Mixed Practice**\n- Complete questions from all topics studied so far.\n- Use timed practice if preparing for an exam.\n\n**Day 11 — Fix Weaknesses**\n- Revisit your error log.\n- Redo questions you previously got wrong.\n- Identify patterns in your mistakes.\n\n**Day 12 — Mock Test or Project Session**\n- Take a practice test under realistic conditions, or work on a major assignment section.\n- Check your work and note areas to improve.\n\n**Day 13 — Final Review**\n- Use active recall: close your notes and explain key ideas from memory.\n- Review formulas, vocabulary, dates, processes, or code patterns.\n\n**Day 14 — Light Review & Rest**\n- Review only the most important material.\n- Prepare materials for your exam, assignment, or next lesson.\n- Get enough rest.\n\n### Daily Study Structure\n1. **5 minutes:** Set a small goal.  \n2. **35–45 minutes:** Focused learning.  \n3. **10 minutes:** Break.  \n4. **20–30 minutes:** Practice and active recall.  \n5. **5 minutes:** Record mistakes and plan tomorrow’s task.  \n\nFor a more personalized plan, share the **subject, exam/assignment date, your current level, and how many hours you can study each day**.',989,'2026-07-22 17:14:22'),(65,20,'user','Help me understand my course',NULL,'2026-07-22 17:21:01'),(66,20,'assistant','Your course, **Python Programming: Zero to Projects**, is designed to help you build Python skills from the basics through practical projects.\n\n### What you’ll learn\nYou’ll practice how to:\n\n- Write and understand Python syntax\n- Use variables, conditions, loops, and functions\n- Work with data structures such as lists and dictionaries\n- Test and debug your code\n- Apply Python to realistic automation and project tasks\n\n### Your current lesson: Core Skills Challenge\nThis lesson is less about learning one new Python command and more about practicing a professional problem-solving process.\n\nThe key workflow is:\n\n1. **Identify the goal**  \n   Ask: *What should the program accomplish?*\n\n2. **Identify the available information**  \n   Ask: *What inputs, data, or requirements do I have?*\n\n3. **Define success criteria**  \n   Ask: *How will I know my solution works correctly?*\n\n4. **Break the work into small, testable steps**  \n   Instead of building everything at once, create and test one part at a time.\n\n5. **Use feedback and improve**  \n   Test your work early, notice problems, then revise it.\n\n6. **Document decisions and assumptions**  \n   Write down important choices—for example: “I assume the user enters a whole number.”\n\n### Small Python example\nGoal: create a program that tells a student whether they passed.\n\n```python\nscore = 72\n\nif score >= 50:\n    print(\"Passed\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Not passed\")\n```\n\nWhy it works:\n\n- `score` stores the student’s mark.\n- The `if` statement checks whether the score is at least `50`.\n- Python prints the correct result based on that condition.\n\nA possible improvement after feedback: let the user enter their own score instead of using a fixed value.\n\n```python\nscore = int(input(\"Enter your score: \"))\n\nif score >= 50:\n    print(\"Passed\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Not passed\")\n```\n\nFor this lesson, try choosing a small real-world task, stating its goal and inputs, writing a short Python solution, and then describing one improvement you would make after testing it.',1044,'2026-07-22 17:21:06'),(69,22,'user','Create concise study flashcards for this lesson. Format each as “Front:” and “Back:”.',NULL,'2026-07-22 23:05:31'),(70,22,'assistant','Front: What is the main goal of this course?  \nBack: Build responsive, accessible websites using modern HTML, CSS, and JavaScript.\n\nFront: How should complex work be approached?  \nBack: Break it into observable, testable steps.\n\nFront: Why use feedback early in a project?  \nBack: It helps identify issues and improve the work before the final result.\n\nFront: What should you document during your work?  \nBack: Important decisions and assumptions.\n\nFront: What should you identify before starting a task?  \nBack: The goal, available information, and quality criteria for success.\n\nFront: What makes a step “testable”?  \nBack: You can check whether it was completed correctly using clear criteria.\n\nFront: What is the practice activity for this lesson?  \nBack: Create a short example, explain why it works, and identify one improvement based on feedback.\n\nFront: Why are assumptions important to document?  \nBack: They make your reasoning clear and help others understand or review your decisions.\n\nFront: What is a quality criterion?  \nBack: A standard used to judge whether the result successfully meets the goal.',827,'2026-07-22 23:05:36'),(71,23,'user','Create concise study flashcards for this lesson. Format each as “Front:” and “Back:”.',NULL,'2026-07-22 23:05:52'),(72,23,'assistant','Front: What is the main goal of this course?  \nBack: Build responsive, accessible websites using modern HTML, CSS, and JavaScript.\n\nFront: How should complex work be approached?  \nBack: Break it into observable, testable steps.\n\nFront: Why use feedback early in a project?  \nBack: It helps identify and fix issues before the final result.\n\nFront: What should you document while working?  \nBack: Important decisions and assumptions.\n\nFront: What should you identify before starting a task?  \nBack: The goal, available information, and quality criteria.\n\nFront: What are quality criteria?  \nBack: Clear standards used to decide whether a result is successful.\n\nFront: What does the practice activity ask you to do?  \nBack: Create a short example, explain why it works, and name one improvement after feedback.\n\nFront: Why is identifying assumptions useful?  \nBack: It makes your reasoning clear and helps others review or improve the work.',793,'2026-07-22 23:05:55');
